\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the original connectome using $\pm $ weights. Increasing sign flip percentage shifts the network from a low-variance, invariant regime toward a higher-performance but less invariant regime. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{15}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bin_cel_frac_curves}{{3}{15}{IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the original connectome using $\pm $ weights. Increasing sign flip percentage shifts the network from a low-variance, invariant regime toward a higher-performance but less invariant regime. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the ER random graph using binary weights. Increasing sign flip percentage shifts the network from a low-variance, invariant regime toward a higher-performance but less invariant regime. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{15}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:er_frac_curves}{{4}{15}{IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the ER random graph using binary weights. Increasing sign flip percentage shifts the network from a low-variance, invariant regime toward a higher-performance but less invariant regime. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the absolute value of the \textit  {C.\ elegans} connectome. The black point represents the negative sign percentage observed in the original connectome. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{16}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:og_cel_frac_curves}{{5}{16}{IPC is denoted in orange, GR in green, MC in blue, and KR in red. Mean computational coefficient of variation and mean performance are shown as a function of the fraction of synapse signs flipped from the absolute value of the \textit {C.\ elegans} connectome. The black point represents the negative sign percentage observed in the original connectome. A total of 250 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.5}{}}
